window.docContents[131].resolve({"/rules/RG1004/#RG1004-rationale":{"contents":"Compiled evaluation is outside the kernel's checking. A compiler or runtime defect could make a false proposition \"proved\". Compiler-trusting is therefore not one of the three logical labels and never counts as conforming evidence.\n\n","context":"Regula rule reference\u0009Rule index\u0009RG1004: Compiler-trusting proofs require separate classification","header":"Why it matters","id":"/rules/RG1004/#RG1004-rationale"},"/rules/RG2003/#RG2003-rationale":{"contents":"Warnings often mark real defects (unused hypotheses, deprecated semantics, unreachable cases). Treating them as failures keeps the elaborated statements exactly those the author intended, and prevents a local option from changing what conformance means.\n\n","context":"Regula rule reference\u0009Rule index\u0009RG2003: Claimed source must elaborate warning-free","header":"Why it matters","id":"/rules/RG2003/#RG2003-rationale"},"/rules/RG3002/#RG3002-rationale":{"contents":"@[implemented_by g] def f makes the kernel reason about f while compiled code runs g. Without a proof relating them, theorems about f say nothing about the program's behavior.\n\n","context":"Regula rule reference\u0009Rule index\u0009RG3002: Checked execution requires admitted correspondence","header":"Why it matters","id":"/rules/RG3002/#RG3002-rationale"},"/rules/RG4001/#RG4001-rationale":{"contents":"A misspelled or misplaced marker must not silently turn a negative example into a positive one or hide a fence from checking. Fail-closed structure keeps every documented Lean claim checked as intended.\n\n","context":"Regula rule reference\u0009Rule index\u0009RG4001: Documentation fences must have a valid classification","header":"Why it matters","id":"/rules/RG4001/#RG4001-rationale"}});